WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.780
A whole nerve is a tangle of many fibres.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.060
So no one could say what one sensory ending sent to the brain.

3
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.080
What does one neuron do, in time, when a stimulus, movement or reward changes?

4
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.300
Recording from inside a cell answers that, but only for a cell you can impale.

5
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.840
This method asks it from outside, in intact tissue.

6
00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:34.843
Active neurons produce currents across their membranes that can be measured in the

7
00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:40.943
fluid outside. So a fine electrode placed near a neuron records its action

8
00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:43.183
potentials without entering the cell.

9
00:00:44.363 --> 00:00:49.023
Hubel and Wiesel used sharpened tungsten wire, insulated with lacquer.

10
00:00:49.843 --> 00:00:55.223
In a conscious person, a tungsten needle goes through the skin into a nerve and is

11
00:00:55.223 --> 00:00:58.823
moved by hand until the activity of interest is isolated.

12
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:03.117
Spikes from one cell have a characteristic shape.

13
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:06.557
So spikes can be grouped by waveform into units.

14
00:01:07.357 --> 00:01:12.297
This step is called spike sorting: decoding the spike times of the recorded neurons

15
00:01:12.297 --> 00:01:13.377
from the raw data.

16
00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:21.077
In a Purkinje cell, simple and complex spikes differ in shape, so one record gives both.

17
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:27.267
Stimuli or events are repeated many times, and spikes are aligned to them.

18
00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:32.747
Each repeat is one row of a raster, with one tick for each spike.

19
00:01:33.707 --> 00:01:39.187
A peristimulus time histogram stacks the repeats, aligned to stimulus onset.

20
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:44.927
It divides each bin's spike count by the number of repeats times the bin width,

21
00:01:45.307 --> 00:01:46.547
giving spikes per second.

22
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:53.750
A tuning curve plots the mean rate against one stimulus value, such as bar

23
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.630
orientation, with many trials per value.

24
00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.910
It is compared with the spontaneous rate.

25
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:06.970
In this idealised cell, a bar at 90 degrees gives 40 spikes a second.

26
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.590
At 30 and 150 degrees, only 5 to 6.

27
00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.070
With no bar, 2.

28
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:20.913
The method samples only a fraction of cells, and favours large, active cells.

29
00:02:21.713 --> 00:02:26.713
In one hippocampal estimate, a tetrode could record signals from about a thousand

30
00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:33.273
neurons. Only 60 to 100 should be detectable, and about 6 units were usually found.

31
00:02:34.113 --> 00:02:38.553
The control is to pair it with imaging, which does not need spikes to be large.

32
00:02:39.293 --> 00:02:42.153
A unit may also hold spikes from two neurons.

33
00:02:42.153 --> 00:02:48.273
Because a neuron has a refractory period, spikes too close together signal contamination.

34
00:02:48.817 --> 00:02:57.297
Hubel and Wiesel recorded single cells in cat in 1962, and in monkey in 1968.

35
00:02:58.457 --> 00:03:04.497
Mapped with moving bars, cat cells fell into simple and complex classes, and monkey

36
00:03:04.497 --> 00:03:10.137
cells into simple, complex and hypercomplex, with orientation selectivity.

37
00:03:11.047 --> 00:03:17.717
In 1971, O'Keefe and Dostrovsky recorded single units in a freely moving rat's

38
00:03:17.717 --> 00:03:22.577
hippocampus. Some cells fired when the animal was at particular places.

39
00:03:23.617 --> 00:03:30.197
In 1994, Mirenowicz and Schultz found that three quarters of recorded monkey

40
00:03:30.197 --> 00:03:33.477
dopamine neurons fired to unpredicted liquid.

41
00:03:34.237 --> 00:03:37.497
Once a cue predicted it, the response moved to the cue.

42
00:03:38.537 --> 00:03:46.017
In 1979, Johansson and Vallbo grouped 334 units from the human median nerve into

43
00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:48.857
four classes by firing and receptive field.

44
00:03:48.857 --> 00:03:55.817
Keep this rule: a recording shows firing goes with an event, never that firing causes it.
